 # VDN-NeRF
 
-Also called: Ours, View-Dependence Normalization, View-Dependence Normalization NeRF
+Also called: View-Dependence Normalization, View-Dependence Normalization NeRF, Ours
 
 ## Overview
-VDN-NeRF is a method to train neural radiance fields (NeRFs) for better geometry under non-Lambertian surface and dynamic lighting conditions by normalizing view-dependence through self-distillation of invariant information encoded in the learned NeRFs [S1]. It resolves shape-radiance ambiguity by aligning the degree of view-dependent radiance variation for all scenes, allowing a single optimal capacity for the view-dependent color function to work across scenes [S1]. Unlike methods that explicitly model underlying factors of view-dependence, VDN-NeRF dilutes color variation with a view-invariant signal derived from a depth prediction network [S1].
+VDN-NeRF is a method for training neural radiance fields (NeRFs) to achieve high-quality geometry under non-Lambertian surface and dynamic lighting conditions [S1]. It resolves the shape-radiance ambiguity by normalizing view-dependent radiance variations through self-distillation of invariant features encoded within the NeRF itself [S1]. Unlike methods that explicitly model complex underlying factors of view-dependence (such as specularities or dynamic illumination), VDN-NeRF aligns the view-dependence of different scenes to a uniform level, allowing a single optimal network capacity to ensure good view synthesis and prevent geometric degradation [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** A set of posed images $\{I_k\}$ of a scene.
-*   **Outputs:** A trained scene representation consisting of an SDF network $f$, a radiance function $c$, and a feature function $F$, capable of rendering novel views and high-quality geometry.
+VDN-NeRF builds on [Neural Radiance Fields](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) and [Surface-SDF Representation](10778-surface-sdf-representation.md) by introducing a parallel feature stream and a distillation network to normalize view-dependent color variations [S1].
 
